{:edb/db-before-t 1018 :edb/db-after-t 1019 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534331 50 #inst "2026-10-11T19:17:55.131Z" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186199888 1078 #edb/ref 17592186199889 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186199892 1078 #edb/ref 17592186199894 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186199895 1078 #edb/ref 17592186199898 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186199899 1078 #edb/ref 17592186199901 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186199902 1078 #edb/ref 17592186199904 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203671 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n> For any $a \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, if $4 \\mid a$ and $6 \\mid a$, then $24 \\mid a$." 13194139534331 17592186045417 false] [17592186203671 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $4 \\mid a$ and $6 \\mid a,$ then $24 \\mid a.$" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203671 1074 "By denoting $P(a)$, $Q(a)$, and $R(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a){:} \\quad 4 \\mid a$\n- $Q(a){:} \\quad 6 \\mid a$\n- $R(a){:} \\quad 24 \\mid a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n$\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\:(P \\land Q) \\Rightarrow R$.\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀a ∈ Z, (P∧Q)⇒R) \\mid ≡∃a ∈ Z, ¬((P∧Q)⇒R); ≡∃a ∈ Z, ¬(¬(P∧Q)∨R); ≡∃a ∈ Z, (P∧Q)∧¬R; ≡∃a ∈ Z, P∧Q∧¬R; ≡∃a ∈ Z, (4∣a)∧(6∣a)∧(24∤a)\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n> To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample.\n> In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that\n> $4 \\mid a \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad 6 \\mid a \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad 24 \\not\\mid a$.\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=12$ suffices.\n\n> Let's choose $a =12$.\n> Then, we have that\n> $4 \\mid 12 \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad 6 \\mid 12 \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad 24 \\not\\mid 12$.\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n> So, $a=12$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 false] [17592186203671 1074 "By denoting $P(a),$ $Q(a),$ and $R(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a){:} \\quad 4 \\mid a$\n\n- $Q(a){:} \\quad 6 \\mid a$\n\n- $R(a){:} \\quad 24 \\mid a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\:(P \\land Q) \\Rightarrow R.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)⇒R) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬((P∧Q)⇒R) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬(P∧Q)∨R) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)∧¬R \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧Q∧¬R \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(4∣a)\\,∧\\,(6∣a)\\,∧\\,(24∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n4 \\mid a\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n6 \\mid a  \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n24 \\not\\mid a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=12$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a =12.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n4 \\mid 12\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n6 \\mid 12\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n24 \\not\\mid 12.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=12$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203673 1071 "Consider the following false statement.\n\n> If $2a \\equiv 2b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$, then $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$ for any $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be a pair of integers $a$ and $b$ such that\n$\\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid black]{\\phantom{RRR}}\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\:4) \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid black]{\\phantom{RRR}}\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$.\n\nFind the missing expressions." 13194139534331 17592186045417 false] [17592186203673 1071 "Consider the following false statement.\n\nIf $2a \\equiv 2b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4),$ then $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$ for any $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ \n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be a pair of integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n\\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid black]{\\phantom{RRR} }\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\:4) \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n\\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid black]{\\phantom{RRR} }\\,  (\\textrm{mod}\\:4).\n$$\n\nFind the missing expressions." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203673 1074 "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b){:} \\quad 2a \\equiv 2b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$\n\n- $Q(a,b){:} \\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P \\Rightarrow Q.\n$$\n\nSince this statement is false, its negation is true.\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be a pair of integers $a$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a \\equiv 2b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4) \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a \\not\\equiv b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4).\n$$" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203673 1074 "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b){:} \\quad 2a \\equiv 2b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$\n- $Q(a,b){:} \\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P \\Rightarrow Q\n$$\n\nSince this statement is false, its negation is true.\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n¬(∀a, b ∈ Z, P⇒Q) \\mid ≡∃a, b ∈ Z, ¬(P⇒Q); ≡∃a, b ∈ Z, ¬(¬P∨Q); ≡∃a, b ∈ Z, P∧¬Q; ≡∃a, b ∈ Z, (2a≡2b(mod4))∧(a≢b(mod4))\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n> A counterexample for the above statement would be a pair of integers $a$ and $b$ such that\n> $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a \\equiv 2b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4) \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a \\not\\equiv b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$." 13194139534331 17592186045417 false] [17592186203674 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n> For any $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, if $3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$, then $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$." 13194139534331 17592186045417 false] [17592186203674 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6),$ then $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6).$" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203674 1074 "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b){:} \\quad 3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\n\n- $Q(a,b){:} \\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P \\Rightarrow Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\na \\not\\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b) =(0,2)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na = 0, \\qquad\nb = 2.\n$$\n\nThen, $3a=0$ and $3b=6.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_{3a} \\equiv \\underbrace{6}_{3b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n\\underbrace{0}_{a} \\not\\equiv \\underbrace{2}_{b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=0$ and $b=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203674 1074 "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b){:} \\quad 3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\n- $Q(a,b){:} \\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n$\\forall a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P \\Rightarrow Q$.\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n$¬(∀a, b ∈ Z, P⇒Q) \\mid ≡∃a, b ∈ Z, ¬(P⇒Q); ≡∃a, b ∈ Z, ¬(¬P∨Q); ≡∃a, b ∈ Z, P∧¬Q; ≡∃a, b ∈ Z, (3a≡3b(mod6))∧(a≢b(mod6))$\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n> To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample.\n> In other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that\n> $3a \\equiv 3b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad a \\not\\equiv b\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$.\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting\n\n$$\n(a,b) =(0,2)\n$$\n\nsuffices.\n\n> Let's choose\n> $a = 0, \\qquad b = 2$.\n> Then, $3a=0$ and $3b=6$.\n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\n> Hence, we have that\n> $\\underbrace{0}_{3a} \\equiv \\underbrace{6}_{3b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\underbrace{0}_{a} \\not\\equiv \\underbrace{2}_{b} \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$.\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n> So, the pair $a=0$ and $b=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 false] [17592186203933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419391 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419393 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419392 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419394 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186203935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419390 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204128 1050 420.0 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419364 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419366 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419368 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419382 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419383 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419385 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419387 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419388 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n> For any $a, b ∈ Z$, if $5a≡5b(mod20)$, then $a≡b(mod20)$.\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}.\nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}} $a≡b$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}" 13194139534331 17592186045417 false] [17592186204214 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20),$ then $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=$ {{field-8}} and $5b=$ {{field-9}}. \n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}{{field-10}}{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}{{field-11}}{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-12}} and $b=$ {{field-13}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186204214 1074 "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(4,8)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=20$ and $5b=40.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}≡{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}≢{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=4$ and $b=8$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186205223 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to disprove conditional statements.\n\nSuppose we wish to disprove a conditional statement of the following form:\n$\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)$\n\nTo disprove this statement, we must show its negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n¬(∀x ∈ Z, P⇒Q) \\mid ≡∃x ∈ Z, ¬(P⇒Q); ≡∃x ∈ Z, ¬(¬P∨Q); ≡∃x ∈ Z, P∧¬Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, to disprove the implication $P\\Rightarrow Q$, it's sufficient to find one value of the variable such that $P$ and $\\lnot Q$ are both true. This value of $x$ is a *counterexample* that disproves the original implication.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139534331 17592186045417 false] [17592186205223 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to disprove conditional statements. \n\nSuppose we wish to disprove a conditional statement of the following form:\n\n$$\n\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)\n$$\n\nTo disprove this statement, we must show its negation is true. \n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀x\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to disprove the implication $P\\Rightarrow Q,$ it's sufficient to find one value of the variable such that $P$ and $\\lnot Q$ are both true. This value of $x$ is a *counterexample* that disproves the original implication.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186205277 1064 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n*For any $a \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $2 \\mid a,$ then $4 \\mid a.$*\n\nCounterexample:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that* \n\n$$\n2 \\mid a  \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid a.\n$$\n\n*Let's choose $a = 2.$ Then, we have that*\n\n$$\n2 \\mid 2\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid 2.\n$$\n\n*So, $a=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186205277 1064 "Disprov