8-abd5-08d5ea9f1942" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419391 1074 "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,a<b$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,3a-1<2b+10$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(a<b)\\,∧\\,(3a-1\\ge 2b+10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\na<b\\,\\text{and}\\,3a-1\\ge 2b+10.\n$$" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419347 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419349 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419350 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419351 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419352 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419353 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419360 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419376 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419378 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419379 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419381 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1069 "q-221742" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a\\in Z,$ if $10∣a$ and $15∣a,$ then $150∣a.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ {{field-7}}.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n10{{field-8}}30\\,\\text{and}\\,15{{field-9}}30\\,\\text{and}\\,150{{field-10}}30.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ {{field-11}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1072 #uuid "d1cc0610-ee4b-58b1-8b76-c910ff50a754" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419392 1074 "By denoting $P(a),$ $Q(a),$ and $R(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,10∣a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,15∣a$\n\n- $R(a):\\,150∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)⇒R.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)⇒R) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬((P∧Q)⇒R) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬(P∧Q)∨R) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)∧¬R \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧Q∧¬R \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(10∣a)\\,∧\\,(15∣a)\\,∧\\,(150∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n10∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,150∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=30$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=30.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n10∣30\\,\\text{and}\\,15∣30\\,\\text{and}\\,150∤30.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=30$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419361 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419377 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419393 1069 "q-221724" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419393 1071 "Consider the following false statement.\n\nIf $3∣a,$ then $6∣a$ for any $a\\in Z.$\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419393 1072 #uuid "d7920e3b-ba93-57c2-9dd2-0e80e521ff6c" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419393 1074 "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,3∣a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,6∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(3∣a)\\,∧\\,(6∤a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n3∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,6∤a.\n$$" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419355 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419357 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419362 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419365 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419367 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419370 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419371 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419373 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419374 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1069 "q-221738" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a\\in Z,$ if $15∣6a,$ then $15∣a.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ {{field-6}}.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n15{{field-7}}{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}\\,\\text{and}\\,15{{field-8}}{{5}_{⏟}}_{a}.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1072 #uuid "db63c74b-9d62-5b22-9fe0-003a0ca7d1b1" 13194139534331 17592186045417 true] [17592186419394 1074 "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,15∣6a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,15∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(15∣6a)\\,∧\\,(15∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n15∣6a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=5$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=5.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n15∣{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤{{5}_{⏟}}_{a}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534331 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-00c3b7a9-db10-52c0-b790-08bed5ce4985" 17592186419242 "answer-061a239b-55c7-5ead-ab36-8bc7d7086e40" 17592186419243 "answer-06b56fba-1fe8-5018-a849-c9135a952109" 17592186419244 "answer-07de8119-b924-5e9d-8b60-11c1ef74d479" 17592186419245 "answer-081aec51-b878-521d-9070-858e6485451b" 17592186419246 "answer-